-- title:
--   Signed products of $2\times 2$ basis matrices attached to band columns
-- statement:
--   All matrices are complex $2\times2$ matrices with rows and columns indexed by $\{0,1\}$; $I$ is the identity and $\operatorname{adj}(X)$ the adjugate, $\operatorname{adj}\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}d&-b\\-c&a\end{pmatrix}$. For $r\in\mathbb{N}$, $S_r$ is the symmetric group on $\{0,\dots,r-1\}$. The file introduces:
--
--   1. **Basis matrices.** For $i\in\mathbb{N}$,
--   $$
--   B_0=I,\qquad B_1=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad B_2=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad B_i=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix}\ \ (i\ge3).
--   $$
--   2. **Parity twist.** For $q\in\mathbb{N}$, $D_q(X)=\operatorname{adj}(X)$ if $q$ is even and $D_q(X)=X$ if $q$ is odd.
--   3. **Segments.** For $r,p\in\mathbb{N}$,
--   $$
--   \Sigma(r,p)=\sum_{\pi\in S_r}\operatorname{sgn}(\pi)\,D_{p}\big(B_{\pi(0)}\big)\,D_{p+1}\big(B_{\pi(1)}\big)\cdots D_{p+r-1}\big(B_{\pi(r-1)}\big),
--   $$
--   an ordered product (for $r=0$ the empty product gives $\Sigma(0,p)=I$). The index $p$ is the global position of the first factor, whose parity decides which factors are replaced by adjugates.
--   4. **Blocks.** For $p\in\mathbb{N}$ and a list $(r_1,\dots,r_k)$ of natural numbers,
--   $$
--   \mathcal B(p;r_1,\dots,r_k)=\Sigma(r_1,p)\,\Sigma(r_2,p+r_1)\cdots\Sigma(r_k,p+r_1+\cdots+r_{k-1}),
--   $$
--   with $\mathcal B(p;\,)=I$ for the empty list: consecutive segments keep their global position parities.
--   5. **Scaled basis matrices.** A matrix $X$ is a scaled basis matrix if $X=c\,B_i$ for some $c\in\mathbb{C}$ with $c\neq0$ and some $i\in\{0,1,2,3\}$.
--   6. **A conjugating matrix.**
--   $$
--   Q=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix},\qquad Q'=\begin{pmatrix}2&-1\\-1&1\end{pmatrix},
--   $$
--   where $Q'=Q^{-1}$.
--
--   In the surrounding development these matrices model the contribution of the columns of a Young diagram with at most four rows when a polytabloid is paired with the band word of width two: after conjugation by the matrix $Q$ of item 6, a column of length $r$ contributes a segment $\Sigma(r,p)$. They are used to prove a noncancellation statement, namely that for every list of column lengths between $1$ and $4$ the entry $\big(Q\,\mathcal B(1;r_1,\dots,r_k)\,Q^{-1}\big)_{00}$ is nonzero, which in turn shows that every Specht module with at most four rows occurs in the cyclic module generated by the band word.
--
--   **Formalization Note** These definitions live in the namespace `Saxl.Band`: `Mat` abbreviates the type of complex $2\times2$ matrices, and the objects are `basis`, `D`, `segment` (the ordered product is `List.ofFn` followed by `List.prod`), `blocks` (defined by recursion on the list), `ScaledBasis`, `Q` and `Qinv` (the matrix $Q'$; that it inverts $Q$ is checked by a separate lemma).
